From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 17:13:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132: Add the CPU energy model

Add opp-microwatt to every OPP of both T8132 (M4) CPU clusters, so the
OPP core registers an energy model and the scheduler can use energy-aware
placement. Without it, EAS stays off on T8132 even though the cpufreq
driver already registers the model from the OPP table.

The values were measured on a MacBook Air (J713) with the method used for
T8140: one busy core per cluster at each operating point, pinned with
scaling_min/max_freq, read from the SMC core-rail power meters.

- P cluster: PP0b with one busy core. The rail voltage steps with the
  P-state (584 mV at 912 MHz) and no other rail rises with the load. Each
  point is the mean of three runs (within 2%), with the top points
  measured from a die below 55 C.
- E cluster: PPbb idle plus a sixth of the rise with all six cores busy,
  as PPbb does not register a single busy core. Below 1476 MHz it does
  not register six either; the 900 and 1080 MHz values use the measured
  rail voltage and the cluster's dynamic coefficient fitted from
  1476-2892 MHz (72 mW per GHz V^2, within 6% at each measured point).
- 4416 and 4464 MHz are turbo states, measured with boost enabled.

With one busy core, 4044 and 4104 MHz run no faster than 3984 MHz, and
3876 MHz draws more than 3936 MHz. The energy model marks these states
inefficient and cpufreq skips them.

On J713 with this model, a medium background load (four threads at 30%
duty) stays on the E cluster with EAS, instead of reaching the P cluster
3-9% of the time without it, and uses 20-57 mW less system power.

Signed-off-by: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
(cherry picked from commit dc2fae850d0bc6ba7594d88d775c99874c708212)
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
